    { "id": "genitive", "title": "The genitive: 'of' (τοῦ, τῆς)", "concept": "cases", "body": [
      "The genitive means 'of': **ἡ οἰκία τοῦ πατρός** 'the house of the father, the father's house'. Endings: **-ου** (2nd decl.), **-ας / -ης** (1st), **-ος** (3rd: πατρ**ός**, παιδ**ός**). Article: **τοῦ** (m/n), **τῆς** (f); plural **τῶν**.",
      "Latin: domus patr**is**, fīlia mātr**is**. Greek also likes to put the genitive between the article and its noun: **ἡ τοῦ πατρὸς οἰκία**. To ask 'whose?': **τίνος;**"
    ], "examples": [
      { "text": "Ἡ οἰκία τοῦ πατρός.", "en": "The father's house." },
      { "text": "Τίνος ὁ κύων; Τοῦ Νικίου.", "en": "Whose is the dog? Nicias'." }
    ], "drills": [
      { "id": "ge1", "prompt": "Say: the father's house", "answer": "ἡ οἰκία τοῦ πατρός" },
      { "id": "ge2", "prompt": "Say: the mother's son", "answer": "ὁ υἱὸς τῆς μητρός" },
      { "id": "ge3", "prompt": "Ask: Whose is the dog?", "answer": "Τίνος ἐστὶν ὁ κύων;" },
      { "id": "ge4", "prompt": "Say: the children's dog", "answer": "ὁ κύων τῶν παίδων" }
    ], "verified": false },
    { "id": "possessive", "title": "My, your, his: ἐμός, σός, αὐτοῦ", "body": [
      "Two ways to say 'my father': **ὁ ἐμὸς πατήρ** (adjective, between article and noun) or **ὁ πατήρ μου** (genitive of ἐγώ after the noun). 'Your': **ὁ σὸς πατήρ** / **ὁ πατήρ σου**.",
      "'His / her / their' use the genitive of αὐτός: **ὁ πατὴρ αὐτοῦ** 'his father', **αὐτῆς** 'her', **αὐτῶν** 'their'. Greek keeps the article where English drops it: **ὁ ἐμὸς ἀδελφός**, literally 'the my brother'."
    ], "examples": [
      { "text": "Ὁ ἐμὸς ἀδελφὸς πρεσβύτερός ἐστιν.", "en": "My brother is older." },
      { "text": "Ἡ μήτηρ αὐτῆς καλή ἐστιν.", "en": "Her mother is beautiful." }
    ], "drills": [
      { "id": "po1", "prompt": "Say: My father is old.", "answer": "Ὁ πατήρ μου γέρων ἐστίν." },
      { "id": "po2", "prompt": "Ask: Where is your sister?", "answer": "Ποῦ ἐστιν ἡ σὴ ἀδελφή;" },
      { "id": "po3", "prompt": "Say: his mother", "answer": "ἡ μήτηρ αὐτοῦ" },
      { "id": "po4", "prompt": "Say: Our house is small.", "answer": "Ἡ οἰκία ἡμῶν μικρά ἐστιν." }
    ], "verified": false },
    { "id": "present", "title": "Present tense: -ω, -εις, -ει, -ομεν, -ετε, -ουσι(ν)", "body": [
      "**λέγω** I say · **λέγεις** you say · **λέγει** he/she says · **λέγομεν** we say · **λέγετε** you (pl.) say · **λέγουσι(ν)** they say. Infinitive: **λέγειν** 'to say'.",
      "Latin: dīc**ō**, dīci**s**, dīci**t**, dīci**mus**, dīci**tis**, dīcu**nt**. Same idea, related endings. The (ν) on -σι(ν) is added before a vowel or at the end of a sentence; you'll see both."
    ], "examples": [
      { "text": "Ἡμεῖς γράφομεν, ὑμεῖς δὲ ἀκούετε.", "en": "We write, and you listen." },
      { "text": "Οἱ παῖδες παίζουσιν.", "en": "The children are playing." }
    ], "drills": [
      { "id": "pr1", "prompt": "Say: I am writing.", "answer": "Γράφω." },
      { "id": "pr2", "prompt": "Say: We are learning the Greek language.", "answer": "Τὴν Ἑλληνικὴν γλῶτταν μανθάνομεν." },
      { "id": "pr3", "prompt": "Ask several people: What are you saying?", "answer": "Τί λέγετε;" },
      { "id": "pr4", "prompt": "Say: The children are playing.", "answer": "Οἱ παῖδες παίζουσιν." }
    ], "verified": false },
    { "id": "accusative", "title": "The accusative: the object (τόν, τήν, τό)", "concept": "cases", "body": [
      "The direct object takes the accusative: **βλέπω τὸν ποταμόν, τὴν θάλατταν, τὸ δῶρον**. Endings: **-ον**, **-ην / -αν**, **-α** (3rd decl.); plural **-ους**, **-ας**, **-α**. Articles: **τόν, τήν, τό**; **τούς, τάς, τά**.",
      "Latin: videō fluvi**um**, terr**am**, dōn**um**. Greek -ον / -ην is Latin -um / -am. As in Latin, word order is free: **τὴν θάλατταν φιλῶ** puts the focus on the sea."
    ], "examples": [
